course: dictionary
lesson: 5795
title: One word, many machines
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one you need. «An airport is like a huge machine for sorting people, bags and planes.» Here a machine is a whole system of parts working together to do a job, so you can picture the airport as a giant engine.
explain: Look for clues about power and moving parts. A washing machine has moving parts and uses power. A vending machine has a slot for money and gives you a snack. Both are machines, but the clues show you which kind is meant. Always ask: what would this machine do?
explain: In science, a simple machine such as a lever, pulley or ramp makes work easier. «Every one of these machines swaps force for distance or speed; none of them gives energy for free.» This tells you that a machine can help you lift or move something, but it cannot create energy from nothing.
fact: A machine is a thing with moving parts that uses power to do a job, like a washing machine or a sewing machine.
fact: A machine can also be a device that sells or gives things, like a vending machine or a ticket machine.
fact: A simple machine, such as a lever, pulley, wheel and axle, ramp, wedge or screw, makes work easier.
fact: «Every one of these machines swaps force for distance or speed; none of them gives energy for free.»
fact: Never put your hand inside a machine that is switched on, and keep loose hair and sleeves away from it.
exercise: «The children bought a drink from the machine in the corridor.» What kind of machine is this? | A wind-up toy | A sewing machine | A simple machine like a lever | *A device that sells or gives things
exercise: «A lever is a simple machine that makes lifting easier.» What is a simple machine for? | To keep things cold | *To make work easier | To make the work harder | To make noise only
exercise: «Every one of these machines swaps force for distance or speed; none of them gives energy for free.» What does this sentence tell you? | *Machines cannot create energy for free | Machines only work on Sundays | Machines always make energy from nothing | Machines are free to use
test: Two machines each make 50 labels an hour. How many labels do they make together in 2 hours? | 100 | *200 | 400 | 50
test: «Tomorrow the factory machine will make 120 biscuits an hour.» How many biscuits will it make in 3 hours? | 120 | 60 | 240 | *360
exam: In «An airport is like a huge machine for sorting people, bags and planes.», what does machine suggest? | *A large system of many parts working together to do a job | A type of bicycle | A drink you buy from a slot | A small toy with a wind-up key
